The Gravwell LLM ingester is positioned between LLM clients and the provider. Clients address it as an
OpenAI-compatible API; the ingester forwards each request upstream and writes what passed through into
Gravwell.

# **Installation**

Debian / Ubuntu:

```shell
apt install gravwell-llm-ingester
```

Redhat / Rocky:

```shell
yum install gravwell-llm-ingester
```

A standalone shell installer is also available on the Gravwell downloads page. A Docker image for this
ingester is not currently available.

Configuration resides at **/opt/gravwell/etc/gravwell_llm_ingester.conf**, with the customary **.d**
overlay directory alongside it.

# **Minimum configuration**

```shell
[Global]
	Ingest-Secret = "YOUR_INGEST_SECRET"
	Cleartext-Backend-Target = 127.0.0.1:4023
	Log-Level = INFO
	Log-File = /opt/gravwell/log/llm_ingester.log

[Listener "openai"]
	Bind = ":4180"
	Upstream-URL = "https://api.openai.com"
	Protocol = "openai-chat"
	Tag-Name = llm
```

This is sufficient to begin recording. **Bind**, **Upstream-URL**, and **Protocol** are the only required
listener parameters, and **openai-chat** is currently the only supported protocol.

The **Tag-Name** selected here is the value to enter in this kit's **LLM_TAG** configuration macro at
install time. The kit default is **llm**.

# **Options required by this kit**

Two listener options are disabled by default, and two dashboards in this kit remain empty without them:

```shell
	Log-Tool-Calls = true
	Log-Usage = true
```

**Log-Tool-Calls** produces the **response.tool_call** and **request.tool_result** events on which the
**LLM Tool Use** dashboard is built. **Log-Usage** produces the **response.usage** events carrying
**prompt_tokens**, **completion_tokens**, and **total_tokens**, which constitute the entirety of the
**LLM Cost and Token Usage** dashboard.

# **Choosing a log mode**

**Log-Mode** determines how much of each request is written. It is the most consequential setting in the
file, for both storage and privacy.

| Mode | What lands in Gravwell |
|---|---|
| **delta** | Only content that is new in this request. The system prompt is written once per session. **Default, and the appropriate choice for most deployments.** |
| **user** | Only the latest user message. The smallest footprint; response-side context is lost. |
| **full** | Every message in every request. Because chat APIs resend the entire conversation each turn, this duplicates the full history on every request. Suitable for short-term debugging, not for production. |

# **Authorization**

Two independent bearer credentials serve distinct purposes:

```shell
	Client-Authorization = "Bearer TOKEN_YOU_ISSUE_TO_YOUR_CLIENTS"
	Upstream-Authorization = "Bearer YOUR_REAL_PROVIDER_KEY"
```

**Client-Authorization** is verified on inbound requests; a request without it receives a 401.
**Upstream-Authorization** replaces whatever the client supplied when the request is forwarded on.

Configuring both removes the need to distribute the provider key. Clients receive a revocable token,
while the provider key remains in a single file on a single host.

# **TLS**

Listeners are cleartext HTTP unless a certificate is supplied:

```shell
	TLS-Certificate-File = /opt/gravwell/etc/llm-proxy.crt
	TLS-Key-File = /opt/gravwell/etc/llm-proxy.key
```

Upstream verification is a separate setting and is enabled by default.
**Insecure-Skip-TLS-Verify-Upstream=true** exists for self-signed local model servers and must not be
used against a production provider.

# **Sessions**

The ingester groups turns into conversations by hashing messages and comparing each request against a
trailing window of hashes from previous requests. Sessions are partitioned per client IP, capped at 256
concurrent sessions per IP, and expire after **Session-TTL** (default 30m).

Configure a state store for sessions to survive an ingester restart:

```shell
	State-Store-Location = /opt/gravwell/etc/llm_ingester.state
	Session-Match-Window = 10
```

Only hashes are written to that file, never message content.

# **Client configuration**

Any client that speaks the OpenAI chat API is supported. The base URL is
**http://INGESTER-HOST:PORT/v1**.

```shell
export OPENAI_BASE_URL="http://llm-proxy.example.com:4180/v1"
export OPENAI_API_KEY="TOKEN_YOU_ISSUE_TO_YOUR_CLIENTS"
```

curl, OpenCode, Crush, Codex, and Claude Code (through its base-URL override) are all configured in this
manner.

# **Verification**

Direct one request through the proxy, then run the following in Query Studio:

```
tag=$LLM_TAG intrinsic event_type | stats count as Events by event_type | table event_type Events
```

The **intrinsic event_type** declaration is required. The ingester attaches its fields as intrinsic
enumerated values, and the query engine does not permit a reference to one until it has been declared;
the same query without **intrinsic event_type** fails rather than returning nothing. The *Kit Overview*
playbook covers this in full, and the kit's macros supply the declaration.

**request.user_message** and **response.assistant_message** should be present at minimum. If the
additional logging options were enabled, **response.usage** should also be present, along with
**response.tool_call** once a tool has been called. The **Event Types Logged** tile on the
**LLM Traffic Overview** dashboard performs the same check in chart form and is the quickest way to
identify that **Log-Usage** was never enabled.

If no events are present, review the ingester log and confirm that the ingester is connected under
**Systems & Health**.

Continue with **LLM Observability - Enabling Semantic Search**.
